@page

<p>
    Now you know the three parts of a website: HTML for the content, CSS for the style, and C#
    with Razor for the logic. In this lesson you will add an <strong>About Me</strong> page to
    this website.
</p>

<h2>Step 1: Make the file</h2>
<p>Open a new terminal in VS Code and run:</p>
<pre>
type nul &gt; Pages\AboutMe.cshtml
code Pages\AboutMe.cshtml
</pre>
<p>Because the file is called <code>AboutMe.cshtml</code>, its address will be <code>/AboutMe</code>.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Write the page</h2>
<p>Paste this in and change the name, color, and hobbies to your own:</p>
<pre>
@@page
@@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About Me";

    var name = "Your Name";
    var favoriteColor = "blue";
    var hobbies = new[] { "Soccer", "Drawing", "Video games" };
}

&lt;div class="about-me"&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hi, I'm @@name!&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;My favorite color is @@favoriteColor.&lt;/p&gt;

    &lt;h2&gt;Things I like&lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;ul&gt;
        @@foreach (var hobby in hobbies)
        {
            &lt;li&gt;@@hobby&lt;/li&gt;
        }
    &lt;/ul&gt;

    &lt;p&gt;I have @@hobbies.Length hobbies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>@@page</code> makes the file a page you can visit.</li>
    <li>The <code>@@{ }</code> block makes three variables.</li>
    <li><code>@@foreach</code> makes a list item for each hobby.</li>
    <li><code>@@hobbies.Length</code> counts the hobbies.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 3: Visit your page</h2>
<p>Save the file and go to <code>http://localhost:5014/AboutMe</code>.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Add some style</h2>
<p>Add this to the bottom of <code>wwwroot/css/site.css</code>:</p>
<pre>
.about-me {
  border: 3px solid blue;
  padding: 20px;
}
</pre>

<h2>Step 5: Add it to the menu</h2>
<p>
    In <code>Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml</code>, add this line just before
    <code>&lt;/nav&gt;</code>:
</p>
<pre>&lt;a href="/AboutMe"&gt;About Me&lt;/a&gt;</pre>

<h2>What to learn next</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Forms</strong> - let people type things into your page</li>
    <li><strong>Databases</strong> - save information so your site remembers it</li>
    <li><strong>JavaScript</strong> - code that runs in the browser</li>
    <li><strong>Publishing</strong> - put your site on the internet</li>
</ul>

<p class="note"><strong>Great job!</strong> You built a website with HTML, CSS, C#, and Razor.</p>
